      "sentences": [
        {
          "id": "S1",
          "text": "I completely agree with your thoughtful approach here for several reasons:\nPer-person numbers are much more relatable - when someone looks at these numbers, they can actually compare it to their own experience (\"I spend about 20 hours a week on housework."
        },
        {
          "id": "S2",
          "text": "\")\nMoving away from \"lazy partner\" to terms like \"primary domestic partner\" or showing percentage splits (60/40, 70/30) is much more constructive and less judgmental."
        },
        {
          "id": "S3",
          "text": "It acknowledges the reality that perfectly equal splits are rare while avoiding blame."
        },
        {
          "id": "S4",
          "text": "Using percentage splits also allows people to self-identify where they fall on the spectrum."
        },
        {
          "id": "S5",
          "text": "Someone might look at it and think \"Oh yes, I do about 70% of the housework\" rather than having to categorize their partner as \"lazy."
        },
        {
          "id": "S6",
          "text": "\"\n\nI'd suggest something like:\nSingle (100%)\nEqual Partnership (50/50 split)\nPrimary/Secondary Partnership (70/30 split)\n\nOr we could even show a range:\nSingle Person Household\nBalanced Partnership (45-55% each)\nUneven Partnership (60-40% to 70-30% split)\n\nThis way people can recognize their situation without feeling defensive or criticized."
        },
        {
          "id": "S7",
          "text": "It's more about understanding patterns than making judgments."
        },
        {
          "id": "S8",
          "text": "Would you like me to create a revised table with this more neutral, per-person approach?"
        },
